# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WorldView configuration
#
# EVERY VALUE HERE IS OPTIONAL. Copy this file to .env and run — with all of it
# left blank you still get a working globe (Cesium's bundled Natural Earth
# imagery) plus live aircraft, road geometry, solar-system ephemerides, DNS and
# IP lookups. Keys switch on additional layers; they are not the price of entry.
#
# TWO KINDS OF VARIABLE, and the difference matters:
#
#   VITE_*   read by the BROWSER. Vite inlines them at BUILD time, so for the
#            production image they must be passed as --build-arg, not just set
#            at run time. Anything inlined is visible to anyone who views the
#            bundle, so only ever put restrictable keys here (see below).
#
#   others   read by the SERVER (the dev middleware, or nginx in the container).
#            These stay out of the browser entirely and are the right home for
#            any credential with a quota or a bill attached.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ── The globe itself ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Google Photorealistic 3D Tiles. Without it the globe still renders, using
# Cesium's bundled offline Natural Earth II imagery — verified, not assumed.
# The honest caveat: Natural Earth is low resolution, so the fallback looks
# right from orbit and goes featureless as you zoom in. Every DATA layer still
# works at any altitude; it is the ground underneath them that loses detail.
# So this key is not decoration — it is what makes close-up views worth having.
# RESTRICT THIS KEY to your domain in the Google Cloud console before deploying
# anywhere public — it is inlined into the bundle and is billable.
# Get one: https://console.cloud.google.com/google/maps-apis/credentials
VITE_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=

# Cesium ion — terrain and hosted assets. Optional; leave blank to run without.
# Get one: https://ion.cesium.com/tokens
VITE_CESIUM_ION_TOKEN=


# ── Layer keys ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# NASA FIRMS — satellite wildfire and thermal-anomaly detections. Free.
# The key travels in the query string, so this one is browser-side by design.
# Get one: https://firms.modaps.eosdis.nasa.gov/api/map_key/
VITE_FIRMS_MAP_KEY=

# WiGLE — crowd-sourced wireless-network density, drawn as a heatmap.
# SERVER-SIDE: the token never reaches the browser. The daily quota is small and
# is charged per ACCOUNT, not per viewer, so the server also rate-limits and
# caches — without that, a handful of open tabs exhausts a day's budget.
# Get one: https://wigle.net/account   (value is the base64 "encoded for use" token)
WIGLE_API_TOKEN=

# AbuseIPDB — IP reputation for the OSINT panel. SERVER-SIDE.
# Get one: https://www.abuseipdb.com/account/api
ABUSEIPDB_API_KEY=


# ── The threat layer ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Coordinates of the host you are defending. They place the "target" marker that
# inbound attack arcs converge on, so set them to YOUR server — the shipped
# example is a datacenter, and pointing arcs at someone else's building is just
# a decoration. Leave blank and the layer quietly does not load.
# This layer also needs THREATS_URL below; without it there is nothing to plot.
VITE_VPS_LAT=
VITE_VPS_LON=


# ── Optional companion services ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Two layers read from services that are NOT public APIs. Standalone, both are
# absent: their routes return 502 and the layers stay empty, which is expected
# and harmless. Point these at your own equivalents to switch them on.

# A relay holding ONE upstream AIS socket and fanning it out to every viewer.
# The upstream is not built to be opened once per browser tab.
# AIS_RELAY_URL=http://ais-relay:9100

# Any Prometheus-compatible store holding intrusion telemetry — this deployment
# feeds it from CrowdSec. Drives the threat layer above.
# THREATS_URL=http://metrics:8428


# ── Not configurable, deliberately ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# OpenSky (aircraft), Overpass (roads), JPL Horizons (solar system), USGS
# (earthquakes), RainViewer (weather radar), CelesTrak (satellites), Cloudflare
# DoH, ipinfo and GreyNoise need no credential at all and are always on.
#
# The MILITARY aircraft layer is the one exception that cannot be switched on
# with a key: since 2026-08 airplanes.live requires prior approval by email
# (contact@airplanes.live) and answers everyone else with 403. OpenSky, which
# backs the civil flights layer, publishes no military filter, so there is no
# drop-in substitute. That layer stays empty and says so once in the console.
